# AiringCal v3 — Deploy Contract (.env.example)
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (git-ignored; NEVER commit or upload `.env`) and fill
# in the values, then drive deploys with:
#   AIRINGCAL_ENV=<env> node scripts/deploy-env.mjs --dry-run   # plan only
#   AIRINGCAL_ENV=development node scripts/deploy-env.mjs --apply
#
# `scripts/deploy-env.mjs` (introduced with this contract, same PR stack) syncs
# GitHub Environment Variables/Secrets and the R2 bucket for the selected
# environment and deploys the readers selected by AIRINGCAL_DEPLOY_TARGET. It is
# dry-run by default when AIRINGCAL_ENV=production.
#
# --- Migration note: staging -> development -----------------------------------
# The old `scripts/setup-staging.sh` flow used `AIRINGCAL_STAGING_*` keys and a
# dual-bucket layout (airingcal-staging-checkpoint + airingcal-staging-public).
# Development now converges on ONE bucket (`airingcal-development`) with key
# prefixes (checkpoints/** private; public/**, snapshots/**, images/** public).
# The legacy staging buckets are the source-of-copy for the migration and are
# NOT deleted by this contract. See the backward-alias table at the bottom:
# existing AIRINGCAL_STAGING_* .env files keep working for development with a
# one-time deprecation warning; do not paste secrets into the new keys until the
# warning has been observed and the new values are confirmed.
# Production bootstrap stays out of scope: never run `--apply` with
# AIRINGCAL_ENV=production without the explicit force flags, and never enable
# AIRINGCAL_WRITER_ENABLED on production by default.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Deploy selector ----------------------------------------------------------
# Environment to configure: development | production.
AIRINGCAL_ENV=development
# Which readers to deploy: cloudflare | edgeone | both.
AIRINGCAL_DEPLOY_TARGET=both

# --- Cloudflare (required input for the R2 + Workers control plane) -----------
# ONE API token is the default Cloudflare credential. Required scopes on the
# target account: Workers R2 Storage Write (bucket admin + r2.dev access),
# Workers Scripts Write (reader deploy), and custom-domain/cache configuration
# for the public bucket host. If any scope is missing, the deploy tool exits 2
# and prints the missing-scope list; it never creates a half-configured bucket.
#
# S3 credentials for reading/writing objects in the bucket are DERIVED from
# this same token by Cloudflare's documented self-derivation: Access Key ID =
# the token `id` (from GET /user/tokens/verify) and Secret Access Key = the
# SHA-256 hash of the token value. No separate R2 API token is created for the
# happy path; the tool verifies the pair with list+head against the target
# bucket, prints only the derived key id (never the secret), and persists the
# pair into the GitHub `development` Environment Secrets for the writer. For
# the happy path you do NOT need to fill the S3 keys below.
# Only if the management token itself lacks R2 object access does the tool
# fall back to creating a dedicated scoped R2 token (stable name
# `airingcal-development-s3`; any prior token with that name is DELETED before the
# replacement is created). That fallback needs the "User API Tokens: Edit
# (Create tokens via API)" scope; without it the deploy exits 2 listing that
# scope plus the override workaround below, rather than half-configuring
# anything.
AIRINGCAL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
AIRINGCAL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# Required for R2 custom domain + host-scoped cache rules + WAF deny of checkpoints/*.
# Without it, --apply aborts attach-custom-domain / cache-rules / waf-checkpoints as
# an explicit missing-capability (zone-wide expressions would affect other sites).
AIRINGCAL_ZONE_ID=

# --- Bangumi writer identity ---------------------------------------------------
AIRINGCAL_BANGUMI_TOKEN=
AIRINGCAL_PRIMARY_USER_ID=

# --- Public bases --------------------------------------------------------------
# Release/base URL served by the bucket custom domain CDN (pointer + images +
# snapshots). r2.dev is disabled after cutover; leave empty to let the deploy
# tool keep the currently verified host.
AIRINGCAL_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
# Image CDN base (optional; DEFAULT OFF). When unset, images reuse
# AIRINGCAL_PUBLIC_BASE_URL. When set it must be an https URL whose host
# DIFFERS from the reader origin, with origin Host/SNI pointed back at the
# public R2 host; the deploy tool validates this and rejects loops.
AIRINGCAL_IMAGE_BASE_URL=

# --- EdgeOne (overseas read project) -------------------------------------------
AIRINGCAL_EDGEONE_API_TOKEN=
AIRINGCAL_EDGEONE_TEAM_ID=
AIRINGCAL_EDGEONE_PROJECT=airingcal-edgeone-read-overseas
AIRINGCAL_EDGEONE_REGION=overseas

# --- Optional S3 overrides (normally leave commented out) ----------------------
# The default is derivation from the Cloudflare API token above (Access Key ID
# = the token `id`, Secret Access Key = SHA-256 of the token value); the
# dedicated scoped token is created only as a fallback. Set these only to
# bypass both paths (e.g. a pre-existing scoped access key is mandated by
# policy). They are overrides, not required fields, and are still verified
# with list+head.
# AIRINGCAL_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AIRINGCAL_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Backward aliases: AIRINGCAL_STAGING_* -> new keys -------------------------
# When AIRINGCAL_ENV=development and a legacy key is present while its new
# counterpart is empty, deploy-env.mjs maps it and prints a ONE-TIME
# deprecation warning naming both keys. Old key names are NOT removed from
# docs/aliases in this change. New keys win when both are set.
#
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN   -> AIRINGCAL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID  -> AIRINGCAL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_BANGUMI_TOKEN          -> AIRINGCAL_BANGUMI_TOKEN
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_PRIMARY_USER_ID        -> AIRINGCAL_PRIMARY_USER_ID
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_PUBLIC_BASE_URL        -> AIRINGCAL_PUBLIC_BASE_URL
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID       -> AIRINGCAL_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY   -> AIRINGCAL_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
#
# Keys with no 1:1 replacement (staging dual-bucket layout only):
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_USERS_JSON, AIRINGCAL_STAGING_PUBLIC_R2_BUCKET,
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_CHECKPOINT_R2_BUCKET, AIRINGCAL_STAGING_S3_ENDPOINT,
#   AIRINGCAL_STAGING_S3_REGION, AIRINGCAL_STAGING_WORKER_NAME — these remain
#   valid inputs to scripts/setup-staging.sh and are used as the migration
#   SOURCE of the copy into `airingcal-development`.
